  "result": "`summarize()` in `src/report.js` now handles 200,000 orders well inside the 1-second target, and `npm test` passes (4 of 4).\n\n**What was slow:** three things, all quadratic.\n- Duplicate orders were checked by scanning an array.\n- Each customer was found by scanning the list of customers.\n- The whole leaderboard was re-sorted after every order.\n\n**What changed:**\n- Duplicate checks use a `Set`.\n- Customer lookup uses a `Map`.\n- The sort runs once, after all orders are processed.\n\nThe ranking rules, the refund and `paid` handling, the EUR conversion, the filter and the limit are untouched. The final order is the same as before because the ranking comparator has no ties between distinct customers.\n\n**Tests added in `test/report.test.js`:**\n- **Comparison with the old code:** a copy of the original implementation is kept as a reference. The test compares results on seeded random data that includes duplicate ids, refunds, EUR orders and tied spend, at several limits.\n- **Speed:** a test runs 200,000 orders and fails if `summarize()` takes 1 second or more. I didn't record the actual time.\n\n**Edge cases:** if `customerId` or `id` values were `NaN` or mixed numbers and strings, the new lookups could behave differently from the old ones. The doc comment says order and customer ids are strings in practice, so I left that alone.",
